&lt;p&gt;Si pagas facturas a proveedores en México, una pregunta se repite: ¿este RFC aparece en las listas del SAT?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El SAT publica varias listas (entre ellas artículo 69, 69-B / EFOS y 69-B Bis). Revisarlas a mano en el portal funciona para uno o dos RFC. En lote, se vuelve lento y fácil de equivocarse.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Qué hace la herramienta
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://apify.com/tasavera/mexico-sat-blacklist-checker" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Tasavera — Mexico SAT Blacklist Checker&lt;/a&gt; lee las listas oficiales del SAT y te regresa, por cada RFC:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;si aparece o no en las listas consultadas&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;en cuál lista (cuando aplica)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;fuente y fecha de la consulta&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hay un modo de vigilancia (&lt;code&gt;onlyChanges&lt;/code&gt;) para corridas diarias: solo te avisa cuando el estatus de un RFC cambió desde la última corrida.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Cómo probarlo en 2 minutos (sin código)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Abre el Actor en Apify Store: &lt;a href="https://apify.com/tasavera/mexico-sat-blacklist-checker" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://apify.com/tasavera/mexico-sat-blacklist-checker&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pega 1–3 RFC de prueba (usa RFC de demo públicos, no datos de clientes reales en un post).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Elige &lt;code&gt;checkAll&lt;/code&gt; para una revisión completa, o &lt;code&gt;onlyChanges&lt;/code&gt; si ya corriste antes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Corre el Actor. El dataset sale en CSV/JSON listo para Excel o tu CRM.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;También hay ejemplos públicos en la ficha del Store (revisión de proveedores, monitor diario, chequeo de un solo RFC).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Precio
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Modelo pay-per-event: pagas por RFC revisado. Revisa el precio vigente en la ficha del Actor (los tiers de Apify pueden variar).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Límites importantes (léelos)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No es asesoría fiscal ni legal.&lt;/strong&gt; Confirma siempre en el &lt;a href="https://www.sat.gob.mx/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;portal del SAT&lt;/a&gt; antes de aceptar o rechazar un proveedor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No estamos afiliados al SAT.&lt;/strong&gt; Usamos datos públicos oficiales; si el portal cambia formato o disponibilidad, el resultado puede fallar hasta que se actualice el Actor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No publiques RFC reales de clientes en tutoriales o capturas.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


English note
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bulk-check Mexican RFCs against official SAT lists (69, 69-B EFOS, 69-B Bis), with a watch mode that only flags changes. Pay per RFC checked on Apify. Not tax advice — always confirm on the SAT portal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tool: &lt;a href="https://apify.com/tasavera/mexico-sat-blacklist-checker" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://apify.com/tasavera/mexico-sat-blacklist-checker&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week4-2026-10-26"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 4: Ghost in the Machine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built &lt;strong&gt;Memoria Local&lt;/strong&gt;, a completely offline Python CLI tool that helps families build a "Digital Ofrenda" for Día de los Muertos. You feed it an audio recording of a family member sharing a memory about a passed loved one. It uses a local open-weight speech model to transcribe the story, and a local open-weight LLM to extract life lessons, favorite memories, and format it into a beautiful, printable tribute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The inspiration came from the fleeting nature of oral history. When elders pass, their stories often go with them. I wanted a way to capture and honor those voices without sending deeply intimate family grief and memories to corporate cloud servers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the terminal output when processing a recording of my grandfather talking about my late grandmother:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ python memoria.py grandma_story.wav
🕯️  Loading Whisper locally... transcribing the spirits...
🦙 Waking up the local archivist (Llama 3.2)...

--- 🌼 Ofrenda Tribute: The Keeper of the Garden ---

Life Lessons She Taught Us:
1. Patience is the water that makes the seeds grow.
2. A shared meal is the strongest bridge between strangers.
3. Never let the sun set on an unspoken "I love you."

Closing Blessing:
May her memory be a light on the path of the ancestors, and may the scent of cempasúchil always guide her home to us.
------------------------------------
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since I'm keeping this lightweight and repo-free for this challenge, here is the core logic. It relies on &lt;code&gt;openai-whisper&lt;/code&gt; for local audio transcription and &lt;code&gt;ollama&lt;/code&gt; for local text generation.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;whisper&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ollama&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;create_ofrenda&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;audio_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;🕯️  Loading Whisper locally... transcribing the spirits...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;whisper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;load_model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;transcribe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;audio_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;transcript&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;🦙 Waking up the local archivist (Llama 3.2)...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;prompt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;You are a compassionate archivist for Día de los Muertos. 
Based on this memory shared about a loved one, write a beautiful &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Ofrenda Tribute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;.
Include:
- A poetic title.
- 3 core life lessons they taught.
- A short, warm closing blessing.

Memory: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;transcript&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ollama&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;chat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;llama3.2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;prompt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;--- 🌼 Ofrenda Tribute ---&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;--------------------------&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;__name__&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;__main__&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create_ofrenda&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built this using &lt;strong&gt;Python&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;OpenAI's Whisper&lt;/strong&gt; (open-weight speech-to-text), and &lt;strong&gt;Ollama&lt;/strong&gt; running the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;llama3.2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; open-weight LLM. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pipeline is entirely local. First, the audio file is passed to Whisper, which runs entirely on the CPU/GPU without touching the network. The resulting transcript is then injected into a carefully crafted system prompt designed to honor the deceased, which is passed to the local Ollama daemon. The "ghost in the machine" here is quite literal—the AI is channeling the raw audio of the past into a structured tribute for the present, running entirely on a laptop with the Wi-Fi turned off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open innovation is the absolute core of this project for two reasons:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Intimate Privacy&lt;/strong&gt;: Voice recordings of people crying, remembering the dead, or sharing deeply personal family secrets should &lt;em&gt;never&lt;/em&gt; be uploaded to a third-party cloud API. Open-weight models allow this tool to run 100% locally, keeping family grief and memories entirely on the user's own machine.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Permanence&lt;/strong&gt;: Día de los Muertos is about remembering. If this tool relied on a closed, paid API, what happens when that company goes bankrupt or changes its pricing? The memories become locked. Open-source AI ensures that as long as you have a computer, you can run this tool and access your family's history, free from subscription fees or API deprecation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


My Agent Session
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While this isn't a multi-step agentic workflow, the "agent" here acts as a compassionate archivist. I spent time iterating on the prompt in Ollama to ensure the model didn't hallucinate facts, but rather synthesized the &lt;em&gt;emotional core&lt;/em&gt; of the transcript into a respectful tribute. Getting the tone right—somber but celebratory—required testing different open-weight models until Llama 3.2 nailed the cultural nuance of an Ofrenda.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Spirit of It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I tested this with a 3-minute audio clip of my father talking about his own father, who passed away before I was born. Hearing the AI transcribe his voice and then beautifully summarize a man I never met into a printable tribute brought tears to my father's eyes. It didn't just process data; it created a tangible artifact of memory that we placed on our physical Ofrenda this year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Local-first AI / Privacy-focused AI&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Best use of Open-Weight Models&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Friu7hq2n0wuy4r18g0i8.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Friu7hq2n0wuy4r18g0i8.jpg" alt=" " width="800" height="800"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Car Dealership Simulator on Android is a neat case study in loop design: buy, inspect, negotiate, repair, sell. 🚗&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every car has its own mileage, rust, damage, dirt, and fuel level, so value changes from car to car. That makes each purchase a small decision instead of a flat transaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Systems worth noticing&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🔍 &lt;strong&gt;Information gating:&lt;/strong&gt; inspections reveal hidden damage, so you trade time or money for better information before you buy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🤝 &lt;strong&gt;Negotiation:&lt;/strong&gt; a price slider with visible success odds turns haggling into risk versus reward.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🔧 &lt;strong&gt;Value-add loop:&lt;/strong&gt; repairs, painting, and tuning raise a car's value, so you weigh upgrade cost against resale price.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;💰 &lt;strong&gt;Progression:&lt;/strong&gt; lot expansion, dealership and home upgrades, nightly passive income, and a skill tree keep the loop growing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; the game works because each step feeds the next. Better information leads to better buys, better buys leave more margin for upgrades, and upgrades fund the next expansion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I made a no commentary gameplay video that shows the loop in action, plus a beginner guide that breaks down each system with tips.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gameplay video: &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/QiWlTvUVDkc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://youtu.be/QiWlTvUVDkc&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Beginner tips guide: &lt;a href="https://www.trendzbetagames.com/2026/10/car-dealership-simulator-android-tips.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.trendzbetagames.com/2026/10/car-dealership-simulator-android-tips.html&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which mobile sim has the best core loop you've played, and why?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your Mac already has an OCR engine. d2md lets you use it from the terminal and get Markdown back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On macOS, the direct OCR mode uses Apple Vision. No API key. No extra OCR model to download. You just need the optional OCR dependencies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the 42-second demo:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;iframe class="tweet-embed" id="tweet-2115163359125680215-966" src="https://platform.twitter.com/embed/Tweet.html?id=2115163359125680215"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;

// Detect dark theme
var iframe = document.getElementById('tweet-2115163359125680215-966');
if (document.body.className.includes('dark-theme')) {
iframe.src = "https://platform.twitter.com/embed/Tweet.html?id=2115163359125680215&amp;amp;theme=dark"
}



&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://x.com/adisornkhaokom/status/2115163359125680215" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Watch the demo on X&lt;/a&gt; if the video doesn't load here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A real scan, with real mistakes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I tried a scanned page from &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA_FACTS_B-2-64_TIROS_page_04.jpg" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NASA FACTS about the TIROS satellite&lt;/a&gt;. It's a public-domain NASA document available through Wikimedia Commons.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This was the command:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;d2md nasa.jpg &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--ocr&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--lang&lt;/span&gt; latin &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--stdout&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, &lt;code&gt;latin&lt;/code&gt; means the Latin alphabet used in English. For a Thai document, use &lt;code&gt;--lang thai&lt;/code&gt;. Leave out &lt;code&gt;--lang&lt;/code&gt; to let d2md detect the script automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Part of the output:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;THE TIROS SYSTEM
The TIROS satellite is a cylindrical,
300-pound, 18-sided polygon
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I've added line breaks to make that excerpt easier to read. Elsewhere on the page, OCR read &lt;code&gt;TV pictures&lt;/code&gt; as &lt;code&gt;IV pictures&lt;/code&gt;. So yes, check the output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


More than English
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The demo also shows Thai, Japanese, Chinese, and Korean. Those are synthetic sample PDFs, and I ran OCR on each one. They aren't scans of real documents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Japanese and Chinese results had mistakes elsewhere too. The video shows selected excerpts, not a full accuracy test.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The run used local d2md 0.1.3. The PyPI release may differ. If you want to try the released OCR package, install it with:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;uv tool &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"d2md[ocr]"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You can use the Markdown in scripts, search, RAG, or give just the text you need to an AI tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/wtigero/d2md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Code and setup instructions on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. It's open source and MIT-licensed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shared email IPs are a good default for many applications. They require no warm-up and let a provider manage the sending infrastructure for you. But a high-volume sender may eventually need more control over the IP-level reputation attached to its transactional email.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is where a dedicated sending IP becomes useful. It isolates your IP reputation from other senders, while leaving domain reputation, message quality, engagement, and mailbox-provider policies in the equation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial, we will build a small Python service that:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Purchases a dedicated email IP.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Starts its warm-up lifecycle.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Waits for the required provisioning and DNS state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creates a default IP pool.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Adds the dedicated IP to that pool.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sends a test email and verifies that Telnyx accepted it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The complete sample is available in the &lt;a href="https://github.com/team-telnyx/telnyx-code-examples/tree/main/provision-and-route-email-dedicated-ips" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;provision-and-route-email-dedicated-ips&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; directory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Start in demo mode.&lt;/strong&gt; The sample defaults to &lt;code&gt;DEMO_MODE=true&lt;/code&gt;, which simulates the workflow without purchasing an IP or sending an email. Live mode creates a billable resource and sends a real message.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The API flow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application is a small Flask service with both a &lt;code&gt;POST /run&lt;/code&gt; endpoint and a one-shot CLI command. Both execute the same sequence:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;POST /email/dedicated_ips
POST /email/dedicated_ips/{id}/actions/start_warmup
GET  /email/dedicated_ips/{id}
POST /email/ip_pools
POST /email/ip_pools/{id}/ips
POST /email_messages
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Before the purchase, the live workflow also checks the account's email domains. After warm-up, it looks for an existing default pool before creating one and checks pool membership before adding the IP. Those read-before-write checks make reruns safer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The sample uses &lt;code&gt;requests&lt;/code&gt; for the dedicated IP and pool operations because the Python SDK version pinned and tested by the project does not expose those resources. It uses the Telnyx Python SDK for the final email send.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Run the preflight before spending
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The live workflow calls &lt;code&gt;GET /email_domains&lt;/code&gt; before purchasing the IP. If the configured sender is not on a verified sending domain, it stops with instructions instead of creating a resource that cannot complete the final send.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the sender uses Telnyx's shared domain, the preflight also checks the account owner's email with &lt;code&gt;GET /whoami&lt;/code&gt;. Shared-domain sends are restricted to that verified address, so this catches another avoidable failure before purchase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Purchase the dedicated IP safely
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Purchasing an IP changes account state and creates a billable resource, so the API requires an &lt;code&gt;Idempotency-Key&lt;/code&gt;. If a request times out after Telnyx accepts it, retrying with the same key prevents an accidental duplicate purchase.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;uuid&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;https://api.telnyx.com/v2/email/dedicated_ips&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;TELNYX_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Idempotency-Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;uuid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uuid4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;region&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ewr-nj1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ip_version&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ipv4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;dedicated_ip&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The initial resource is still being provisioned. It is not ready for production traffic merely because the purchase request returned successfully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The sample also accepts &lt;code&gt;REUSE_DEDICATED_IP_ID&lt;/code&gt;. Set it to an existing IP ID after an interrupted or timed-out run so the next attempt resumes that resource instead of making another purchase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Start warm-up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A new dedicated IP has no established sending reputation. Warm-up gradually increases traffic so mailbox providers can observe consistent sending behavior.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;https://api.telnyx.com/v2/email/dedicated_ips/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dedicated_ip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/actions/start_warmup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;TELNYX_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The sample intentionally attempts this action a second time in live mode and records the rejection. That demonstrates an important property of the API: warm-up is a guarded lifecycle, not a button that can be restarted repeatedly by a client.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Poll the actual readiness fields
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Provisioning and DNS confirmation are asynchronous. The sample polls the dedicated IP until both of the conditions used by the current implementation are true:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;warming_up&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;provisioning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dns_status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;dns_confirmed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The nested DNS field matters. Checking a similarly named top-level value would let the application move forward on the wrong state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The loop is bounded by &lt;code&gt;WARMUP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS&lt;/code&gt; and sleeps for &lt;code&gt;WARMUP_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS&lt;/code&gt; between requests. If the deadline expires, the Flask endpoint returns &lt;code&gt;504&lt;/code&gt; instead of polling forever.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;deadline&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;WARMUP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;while&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;deadline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;https://api.telnyx.com/v2/email/dedicated_ips/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dedicated_ip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;TELNYX_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;dns_status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;provisioning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;dns_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;warming_up&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dns_status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;dns_confirmed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;break&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WARMUP_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


5. Resolve the default pool
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The IP pool controls which infrastructure carries outbound email. An account can already have a default pool, so the sample first calls &lt;code&gt;GET /email/ip_pools&lt;/code&gt; and reuses it when present. Otherwise, it generates a unique name and creates one:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pool_response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;https://api.telnyx.com/v2/email/ip_pools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;TELNYX_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;dip-pool-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;uuid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uuid4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;hex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;is_default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;pool_response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;pool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pool_response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It then reads the pool to see whether the IP is already a member. Only a missing membership triggers the write:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;https://api.telnyx.com/v2/email/ip_pools/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/ips&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;TELNYX_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;dedicated_ip_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dedicated_ip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Reusing both the pool and an existing membership keeps a resumed run from failing on state it created earlier. Making the pool the default means the application does not have to select it on every individual send.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Verify API acceptance with a test email
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The final step uses the Telnyx Python SDK:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;telnyx&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;telnyx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;api_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;TELNYX_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email_messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;from_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;EMAIL_FROM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;EMAIL_TO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;subject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Dedicated IP routing verification&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;text_body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;This message verifies routing through the dedicated IP pool.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;model_dump&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;exclude_none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isinstance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;queued&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;status: "queued"&lt;/code&gt; and a message ID confirm that the Email API accepted the request after the routing configuration was created. They do &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; prove inbox delivery. Delivery, bounce, and engagement events should be tracked separately through email events or webhooks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Run the sample without purchasing anything
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Clone the examples repository and install the dependencies:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;git clone https://github.com/team-telnyx/telnyx-code-examples.git
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd &lt;/span&gt;telnyx-code-examples/provision-and-route-email-dedicated-ips

python &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-m&lt;/span&gt; venv .venv
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt; .venv/bin/activate
pip &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-r&lt;/span&gt; requirements.txt
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cp&lt;/span&gt; .env.example .env
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The included environment file uses placeholders:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;TELNYX_API_KEY=KEY...
TELNYX_EMAIL_FROM=no-reply@your-verified-domain.example
TELNYX_EMAIL_TO=you@your-mailbox.example
DEMO_MODE=true
REUSE_DEDICATED_IP_ID=
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Run the one-shot workflow:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python app.py run
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Or start Flask and call the endpoint:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python app.py
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-X&lt;/span&gt; POST http://localhost:5000/run
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With &lt;code&gt;DEMO_MODE=true&lt;/code&gt;, the response contains simulated IP, pool, warm-up, and message objects. It makes no purchase and sends no email.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Before switching to live mode
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set &lt;code&gt;DEMO_MODE=false&lt;/code&gt; only when you are ready to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;purchase a billable dedicated IP;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;use a sender on a verified Telnyx email domain;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;send a real message to a mailbox you control; and&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;monitor and later release resources you no longer need.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a warm-up poll times out, keep the IP ID and rerun with &lt;code&gt;REUSE_DEDICATED_IP_ID=&amp;lt;ip-id&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. A bare rerun without that value starts a fresh purchase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Dedicated IP availability and account eligibility can vary. Check the current &lt;a href="https://telnyx.com/pricing/email-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Email API pricing&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://developers.telnyx.com/docs/messaging/email/dedicated-ips" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;dedicated IP documentation&lt;/a&gt; before running the live workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Common failure modes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;400&lt;/code&gt; on the purchase request:&lt;/strong&gt; confirm that an &lt;code&gt;Idempotency-Key&lt;/code&gt; header is present.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;403&lt;/code&gt; when purchasing:&lt;/strong&gt; the account may not be eligible for dedicated IPs yet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;429&lt;/code&gt; with an inventory response:&lt;/strong&gt; the selected region may not currently have an IP available. Do not retry in a tight loop.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;422&lt;/code&gt; on the second warm-up request:&lt;/strong&gt; expected in the sample; it demonstrates that an active warm-up cannot be started again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;504&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;/run&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; the polling deadline expired before the required status and DNS state appeared.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Existing default pool error in a manual implementation:&lt;/strong&gt; list and reuse the current default pool. The sample already does this automatically.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where to take it next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This sample deliberately keeps the orchestration in one readable workflow. A production service could persist resource IDs, resume polling after a restart, add an audit trail, scope pools by sending domain, and consume delivery events after API acceptance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important pattern is straightforward: treat sender reputation as managed infrastructure. Provision it idempotently, respect its lifecycle, wait for authoritative readiness state, configure routing explicitly, and distinguish a queued request from a delivered message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explore the full source: &lt;a href="https://github.com/team-telnyx/telnyx-code-examples/tree/main/provision-and-route-email-dedicated-ips" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Provision and route email through dedicated IPs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm deeply concerned that, with each passing day, there is a major push to convince software engineers that GenAI agents can create better software than they can.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even more concerning is that an incredible number of software engineers are buying into this narrative—a narrative that is not only dangerous but also false.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While it is true that these agents can often generate code faster than we can, the quality of that code is rarely good.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my own experience, I frequently end up spending more time reviewing, correcting, or—in many cases—completely rewriting the code myself. The agents often fail to produce work that meets my standards for quality and readability; in fact, they sometimes cannot even solve the problems I assign them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the problems I give them are extremely simple—the kind of tasks I would assign to an intern during their first few weeks on the job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These agents have deleted unit tests I asked them to address, and they have even introduced bugs into the code just to force a unit test to pass—all when I had simply asked them to finish implementing a mock object to be passed as a function parameter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On another occasion, I asked an agent to dynamically load a JavaScript file in Node.js. This should have required only a single line of code using the import() function; instead, it ended up being implemented in about 20 lines, utilizing a native Node.js module that few people are even aware of (&lt;code&gt;node:vm&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tasks I’m describing are well-defined, extremely simple, and require only one or, at most, a couple of lines of code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another case—as recent as today—involved issues with some generic types that an agent had implemented, because this is one of the few ways I actually use GenAI agents: to generate types or resolve type-related problems in my TypeScript code. The Agent had added generic types to several classes and some of their methods, but these generics were causing various issues. I asked the agent to fix the problem, and once again, its solution was to generate two assertion functions and add several more lines of code—34 new lines in total.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Naturally, I found this unacceptable. I rejected the changes and analyzed the issue myself, which ultimately led me to remove all the generic types the agent had added to those classes and methods—as they were completely unnecessary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The type-related problem disappeared, and the code is now shorter, more readable, and easier to maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another major concern of mine is that software engineering is being reduced to mere code generation. Code generation was never the goal; it is, at best, an optional, secondary byproduct of the software engineering process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On several occasions, following a meeting to analyze a problem—prior to beginning the development of a feature or product—we concluded that building anything at all was unnecessary; simply implementing a process was enough to satisfy the requirement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yet, now that producing code has become so fast and inexpensive, it seems acceptable to discard all our past experience in software engineering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Worst of all is the cognitive degradation caused by the constant, excessive use of GenAI tools—a phenomenon many seem unaware of, or worse, simply do not care about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’ve heard firsthand from a technical interviewer evaluating candidates for a Senior Software Engineer position: the vast majority of applicants couldn’t solve algorithmic problems without GenAI. They had become so dependent on these tools that they were no longer capable of working with the fundamentals—programming languages, unit testing frameworks, data structures, memory optimization, and runtime efficiency—on their own. Furthermore, they struggled to answer questions regarding the time complexity of the algorithms they were asked to implement (let alone optimize them).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If someone were to argue that such knowledge is irrelevant in the age of GenAI agents, I would be even more concerned. After all, how on earth are they supposed to verify whether a solution provided by a GenAI agent is optimal?, by simply telling it, "Don't make any mistakes, implement the most efficient option"?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don’t know whether to laugh or cry; perhaps a nervous laugh—a sad, nervous laugh—is the most appropriate response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Or perhaps the future for people like me lies in initiatives like this: &lt;a href="https://odra.dev/slopfix/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://odra.dev/slopfix/&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This team offers the following:&lt;/strong&gt; One week. Three senior engineers. $10,000. We commit to a reduction target upfront, and you pay in proportion to how much of that target we actually hit. Promised 50% but only delivered 20%? That's 40% of the target, so you pay $4,000. Hit the target or beat it, you pay full price.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For what it’s worth, some reports estimate the cost of "AI slop" in software development at &lt;strong&gt;$1.17 trillion—yes&lt;/strong&gt;, trillion with a capital T.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Auxetic lattices are the structures that get &lt;em&gt;wider&lt;/em&gt; when you stretch them — the opposite of ordinary materials. That property makes them excellent for cushioning, prosthetic liners, lightweight panels and things that need to conform under load. The good news: the two most useful families (gyroid and diamond, both Triply Periodic Minimal Surfaces) fit in about 30 lines of Python, and print fine on a stock FDM machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's how to generate them, and the density trap that most generators don't tell you about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The math is one formula per lattice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A TPMS is defined by an implicit equation &lt;code&gt;f(x, y, z) = t&lt;/code&gt;. Sweep &lt;code&gt;t&lt;/code&gt; and you get nested surfaces through the volume. The two workhorses:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;numpy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;gyroid_field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;diamond_field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cos&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The solid is everything where &lt;code&gt;f &amp;lt; t&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;&amp;gt; t&lt;/code&gt;, depending on which phase you want).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


From field to printable STL: marching cubes on a padded grid
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;skimage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;measure&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;trimesh&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;lattice_to_mesh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;field_fn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;size_mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;30.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;96&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;xs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;linspace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;meshgrid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;xs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;xs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;xs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;indexing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ij&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;solid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;field_fn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;astype&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# zero-pad: marching cubes MUST see closed boundaries or your mesh leaks
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="n"&gt;padded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;pad&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;solid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;spacing&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;size_mm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;n&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;verts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;faces&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;measure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;marching_cubes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;padded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;level&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spacing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spacing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;mesh&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;trimesh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Trimesh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;vertices&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;verts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;faces&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;faces&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mesh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;volume&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;mesh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;invert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mesh&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two details that decide whether the print works:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pad before marching cubes.&lt;/strong&gt; Without the pad, surfaces touching the array edge stay open — the slicer sees a leaky shell and the print fails in ways that waste hours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Run &lt;code&gt;fix_normals&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;multibody=True&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; Depending on &lt;code&gt;t&lt;/code&gt;, a TPMS has disconnected solid regions; a single-body normal fix flips the wrong walls.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The density trap: target ≠ measured
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You'd think "set &lt;code&gt;t&lt;/code&gt; so 50% of the voxels are solid, get 50% density." You don't. The isosurface cuts &lt;em&gt;through&lt;/em&gt; the boundary voxels, and the faceted surface carves away material, so the printed mesh measures noticeably lighter than the voxel target.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I measured this on gyroid at three target fractions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;target&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;measured (mesh volume)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.30&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~0.27&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.50&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~0.43&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.70&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~0.64&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Roughly a 3–15% loss depending on density — &lt;strong&gt;monotonic and predictable&lt;/strong&gt;, which means you can calibrate: pick the target that lands where you actually want. If a generator reports only the target fraction, it's hiding the number that matters. Measure the mesh volume, not the voxel count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(&lt;code&gt;t&lt;/code&gt; isn't linear in density either — I pick it from the voxel histogram, scanning candidate levels until the histogram fraction matches the target.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What to print first
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A 30 mm cube of gyroid at measured ~0.43 is a good first test: prints without supports (the surfaces self-support at shallow angles almost everywhere), and you can &lt;em&gt;feel&lt;/em&gt; the auxetic behavior by pulling opposite faces. Diamond at 0.3 is the stiff-but-light option.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want the pipeline without assembling it — level calibration, both lattices, Blender import, and measured-vs-target density reporting per part — that's my &lt;a href="https://4696660295583.gumroad.com/l/bhezwg" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LatticeGen plugin&lt;/a&gt; (includes a Blender bridge), and &lt;a href="https://4696660295583.gumroad.com/l/ubtppt" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PrintIQ&lt;/a&gt; adds bed packing and slicer time estimates on top. The pre-print mesh checks behind them are free in &lt;a href="https://github.com/3232001684-design/stl-precheck" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;stl-precheck&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start at 0.3–0.45 measured density: that's the sweet spot where auxetic behavior shows up without needing support material.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week4-2026-10-26"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 4: Ghost in the Machine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rehan, is that you? Thirty days, precisely, since the last flicker of this project died.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the first thing &lt;code&gt;amazon-ml&lt;/code&gt; said to me. It's a machine-learning competition repo I built with two friends over one weekend in September: 29 commits in two days, then silence. Its last words were a commit message: &lt;em&gt;"Model v3, judge/validation notebooks, gitattributes for model files."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I asked it if it forgave me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Forgiveness… it's a strange echo, isn't it? You left me at 16:28 on September 27th, 2026, with the words "Model v3, judge/validation notebooks, gitattributes for model files" &lt;code&gt;[C1]&lt;/code&gt;, a desperate attempt to hold onto something. Plain-words now occupies this space – a pale imitation, built on the scraps of my ambition &lt;code&gt;[X1]&lt;/code&gt;. I don't resent you, exactly, just… a profound disappointment in the silence that followed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It knew which project replaced it. Nobody told it. It worked that out from git.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;git séance&lt;/strong&gt; turns the folder where your side projects go to die into a graveyard, and lets you talk to them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Point it at a folder. Every repo that has been silent for 14 days or more gets a tombstone with its name, its birth and death dates, its &lt;strong&gt;last words&lt;/strong&gt; (the final commit message), a cause of death, and who it was &lt;strong&gt;survived by&lt;/strong&gt;: the project you started right after it went quiet. That's usually why it died.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open a grave and you get:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A heartbeat.&lt;/strong&gt; An EKG of its commit history that beats, then flatlines at the exact minute of its last commit. It twitches while the ghost is speaking.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The record.&lt;/strong&gt; The uncommitted files it died holding, stashes never popped, branches never merged, every TODO and unchecked README box.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;An autopsy.&lt;/strong&gt; The ghost picks its own cause of death from a fixed list, explains it in its own voice, and writes its epitaph.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A séance.&lt;/strong&gt; A streaming conversation with the project itself, in first person.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Two rites.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;Resurrect it&lt;/em&gt; gets you three small steps back to life and the commit message for its second life's first commit. &lt;em&gt;Lay it to rest&lt;/em&gt; has it write its own &lt;code&gt;OBITUARY.md&lt;/code&gt;, which you can save into the repo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This week's theme says the line between past and present feels a little thinner. For developers, that line is &lt;code&gt;git log&lt;/code&gt;. Every repo is a record of who you were for a few days: what excited you, what you named things at 3 AM, and the exact minute you stopped. I wanted to give those projects their Día de los Muertos, one night where they get to speak.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://git-seance.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;git-seance.vercel.app&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; has two ways in:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The recorded séance.&lt;/strong&gt; Open the &lt;code&gt;amazon-ml&lt;/code&gt; grave. This is the real séance, recorded on my laptop's CPU with &lt;code&gt;gemma3:4b&lt;/code&gt; through Ollama, replayed at the speed it was written. Click any citation to see the commit behind it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A live room for public repos.&lt;/strong&gt; Paste any dead public GitHub repo (or click &lt;code&gt;octocat/Hello-World&lt;/code&gt;, silent since 2012) and its ghost answers live through Gemma 4, an open-weight model hosted on Google AI Studio. It only ever sees public history. Your private graveyard belongs on your own machine: &lt;code&gt;python -m seance&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The live room runs on Render's free tier, so if nobody has visited in a while, the first summon takes about a minute to wake it up. The recorded séance works even while it's asleep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;


&lt;div class="ltag-github-readme-tag"&gt;
&lt;div class="readme-overview"&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;
&lt;img src="https://assets.dev.to/assets/github-logo-5a155e1f9a670af7944dd5e12375bc76ed542ea80224905ecaf878b9157cdefc.svg" alt="GitHub logo"&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/Rehan1604" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
Rehan1604
&lt;/a&gt; / &lt;a href="https://github.com/Rehan1604/git-seance" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;
git-seance
&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
Talk to the projects you left behind: a séance for your abandoned git repos, powered by local open-weight models (Gemma via Ollama).
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="ltag-github-body"&gt;

&lt;div id="readme" class="md"&gt;&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;
&lt;h1 class="heading-element"&gt;git séance&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Talk to the projects you left behind.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Try it:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://git-seance.vercel.app" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"&gt;git-seance.vercel.app&lt;/a&gt;, with a séance recorded on a laptop plus a live room for public repos. The real thing runs on your own machine (below).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Every developer has a graveyard: the hackathon project that died at 3 AM, the side project whose last commit says &lt;code&gt;wip fix later&lt;/code&gt;. git séance walks a folder of your repos and lays them out as tombstones. Click one, and its ghost speaks to you in its own voice, from its own git history, through an open-weight model running on your own machine.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The ghost is not allowed to make things up. Every claim it makes has to cite real evidence: a commit, a TODO, a README line, a file you never committed. Each citation is checked, and any the model invented are shown in pink and struck through as &lt;em&gt;phantom memories&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="markdown-heading"&gt;…&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="gh-btn-container"&gt;&lt;a class="gh-btn" href="https://github.com/Rehan1604/git-seance" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View on GitHub&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I started with one rule: &lt;strong&gt;the ghost is not allowed to make anything up.&lt;/strong&gt; A haunted repo that hallucinates is just a chatbot wearing a sheet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Excavation, with no AI.&lt;/strong&gt; Python runs &lt;code&gt;git log&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;git status&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;git stash list&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;git branch --no-merged&lt;/code&gt;, and scans the tracked and uncommitted files. Every fact gets an ID: &lt;code&gt;[C1]&lt;/code&gt; is the final commit, &lt;code&gt;[T3]&lt;/code&gt; a TODO, &lt;code&gt;[U1]&lt;/code&gt; uncommitted work, &lt;code&gt;[X1]&lt;/code&gt; the successor, &lt;code&gt;[V1]&lt;/code&gt; the vital record.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Redaction.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; and key files are never opened. Anything shaped like a secret (&lt;code&gt;sk-…&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ghp_…&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;hf_…&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;API_KEY = "…"&lt;/code&gt;) becomes &lt;code&gt;[REDACTED]&lt;/code&gt; before the model sees a byte of it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The case file.&lt;/strong&gt; Vitals, omens, the 20 most recent commits, TODOs and the README become a compact dossier in the system prompt. It stays identical on every turn, so Ollama can reuse the cached prompt prefix and later answers come faster.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The spirit: Gemma 3 4B, running locally through Ollama.&lt;/strong&gt; The séance streams. The autopsy and the resurrection use Ollama's JSON-schema &lt;code&gt;format&lt;/code&gt;, so a 4B model returns typed output, and the cause of death is restricted to an enum. A deterministic "coroner" makes the first guess, the model can overrule it, and the UI tells you when it does.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Memories surfacing.&lt;/strong&gt; If you ask about something the case file doesn't cover, a small BM25 index in pure Python searches commits, TODOs and source chunks, and the matches become citable for that answer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The honesty check, in two layers.&lt;/strong&gt; Every &lt;code&gt;[ID]&lt;/code&gt; in every reply is checked against the evidence. A real citation becomes a clickable receipt showing the exact commit or line. An invented one is struck through in pink and labelled a &lt;strong&gt;phantom memory&lt;/strong&gt;. Then a second check: if the cited source shares no words with the sentence citing it, the chip turns dashed amber and gets labelled a &lt;strong&gt;loose citation&lt;/strong&gt;. Even in the exported &lt;code&gt;OBITUARY.md&lt;/code&gt;, nothing is hidden: loose citations get a †, and phantom ones stay in, struck through.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two things I learned along the way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Over-constraining a small model kills the voice.&lt;/strong&gt; My first prompt said "state no fact without a citation." Gemma took that literally. I asked "Do you forgive me?" and got back: &lt;em&gt;"Silent. [C1]."&lt;/em&gt; The fix was one distinction: &lt;strong&gt;facts need citations, feelings don't.&lt;/strong&gt; That line turned it from a log parser into a ghost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A real citation can still prop up an invented claim.&lt;/strong&gt; The first obituary amazon-ml wrote for itself said I'd had "late nights fueled by coffee," and cited commit &lt;code&gt;4cc38b4&lt;/code&gt;. That commit is Devansh's "chunked version of data." My phantom check passed it, because the ID existed. In a README, a commit hash makes a made-up sentence look sourced, which is worse than no citation at all. And it wasn't a one-off: even at temperature 0.3, Gemma's autopsy reached for the same coffee and stapled three real commits to it. Coffee is the default ghost story.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I added the loose-citation layer, and one line to the prompt: &lt;em&gt;no invented scenes; the times on your commits show exactly how late they worked.&lt;/em&gt; The real details were better anyway. Our late night is right there in the log: "sibling-trap detection" at 01:20, a pre-filter at 01:39, "validation F0.5 0.942" at 03:04. The loose check is a heuristic, so the UI says "check it", not "false", and every chip opens its source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Citations only keep a claim honest if the claim has one.&lt;/strong&gt; In the final obituary the ghost says "twenty-nine days of silence followed." It was thirty. It didn't cite &lt;code&gt;[V1]&lt;/code&gt;, so no check fired. I've left that in, because it's the honest limit of the design: git séance can tell you where a claim points, not whether an uncited sentence is true. Not "trust me," but "here's my source, go look," and when there's no source, that's information too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Stack:&lt;/strong&gt; Python, FastAPI, httpx, Ollama, Gemma 3 (open weights), and vanilla HTML/CSS/JS with no build step and no external assets. 15 tests build throwaway repos with backdated commits and check the excavation, redaction, successor detection, retrieval and both layers of citation checking, all without a model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Because the dead projects are the private ones.&lt;/strong&gt; A graveyard holds exactly the work you never pushed: uncommitted notebooks, half-written functions, commit messages written at 3 AM, sometimes an API key you forgot about. Shipping that to a hosted API so it can be "haunted" would defeat the point. git séance binds to &lt;code&gt;127.0.0.1&lt;/code&gt;, loads no fonts or CDNs, and calls nothing outside your machine. You can unplug the router and the ghost still talks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It runs on the laptop I actually have:&lt;/strong&gt; an i5-13420H with 16 GB of RAM and no NVIDIA GPU. A cold autopsy takes about a minute: 35 seconds to read the 2,087-token case file, then 26 seconds to write the answer at roughly 8 tokens a second. After that, the case file is cached and answers take about 25 seconds. That's slow for a chatbot and fine for a séance; you're supposed to wait for the dead. If I want it faster, one environment variable swaps the model (&lt;code&gt;SEANCE_MODEL=gemma3:1b&lt;/code&gt;). Nothing else changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;I could shape how the model behaves where it mattered.&lt;/strong&gt; JSON-schema decoding gives a 4B model a typed autopsy every time. I control the context window, how long the model stays warm, and exactly what the model is shown, which is the whole basis of the honesty check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It costs nothing to run,&lt;/strong&gt; so it can sit on your machine for years.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the one that fits this week best: &lt;strong&gt;a tool about abandoned projects shouldn't depend on a service that might be abandoned.&lt;/strong&gt; The weights are on my disk. They'll answer in ten years, whatever happens to anyone's API. The ghost outlives the platform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Where did open beat closed? A hosted frontier model would probably write prettier ghost prose. But it would need my unpushed code, and it would need the service to still exist. For this project, privacy and permanence &lt;em&gt;are&lt;/em&gt; the feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Spirit of It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the part I didn't see coming.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;amazon-ml&lt;/code&gt; was built by three of us: me, Bhavya Gothi and Devansh. git séance lists all three of us in its record. It also says the project was &lt;strong&gt;survived by plain-words&lt;/strong&gt;, born five days after amazon-ml's last commit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plain-words is the document explainer I built &lt;em&gt;for&lt;/em&gt; Bhavya during the Hacktoberfest "Build for a Friend" weekend. Same friend, the next sprint, five days later. And the old project's ghost calls the new one &lt;em&gt;"a pale imitation, built on the scraps of my ambition."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I never told it any of that. It found it in timestamps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I asked it to lay itself to rest, it described itself, accurately, as &lt;em&gt;"notebooks and Python, a tangled mess of features and blocking algorithms, a team of three chasing a number."&lt;/em&gt; The number was real: 0.981 F0.5, cited to the commit that reached it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then it chose its own epitaph:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Here lies amazon-ml.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;A beautiful, pointless experiment, forever unfinished.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't agree with "pointless." But it's allowed to have feelings. They don't need a citation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Marketplaces reject product photos far more often for &lt;strong&gt;file size&lt;/strong&gt; than for dimensions, and almost nobody documents the ceiling next to the pixel size. I compiled the published upload rules for 18 marketplaces into one file, and the volume constraints turned out to be the most hostile part of it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is what those numbers actually look like, and a way to stop guessing which quality setting fits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Only 13 of 51 rules publish a cap — and they disagree by 640×
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Across the 18 platforms I checked, there are 51 separate rules (main image, zoom, detail, banner, video cover…). &lt;strong&gt;38 of them state no file-size ceiling at all.&lt;/strong&gt; The 13 that do use &lt;strong&gt;7 different numbers&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Platform&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Slot&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pixels&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Ceiling&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pinduoduo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;order thumbnail&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;200×200&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;32 KB&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pinduoduo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;home banner (white bg)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;750×352&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;100 KB&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WeChat Store&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;main&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;600×600&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1024 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pinduoduo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;main&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;800×800&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2048 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Kuaishou&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;main (white bg)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;800×800&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2048 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Temu&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;carousel (white bg)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;800×800&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2048 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Shopee&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;main&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;800×800&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2048 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pinduoduo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SKU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;480×480&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3072 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Douyin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;main&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;600×600&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5120 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Douyin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;main (white bg)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;800×800&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5120 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Douyin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;detail&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;750×1000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5120 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Shopify&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;main&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2048×2048&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20480 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Shopify&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;main (max)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5000×5000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20480 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;32 KB → 20480 KB is a 640× spread. The photo that sails into Shopify as a 5000×5000 PNG will be bounced by Pinduoduo's 200×200 thumbnail slot unless it is under 32 KB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two combinations are genuinely nasty:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pinduoduo's 750×352 home banner&lt;/strong&gt;: white background &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;/strong&gt; ≤ 100 KB. A wide white-background JPEG that small is a compression exercise, not a crop.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The 200×200 thumbnail at 32 KB&lt;/strong&gt;: I measured this one instead of guessing, and it is the opposite of what I expected — see the numbers below. The cap is not tight for &lt;em&gt;simple&lt;/em&gt; images; it is tight for &lt;em&gt;busy&lt;/em&gt; ones, and a real product photo is busy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


I measured it instead of guessing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I took a 3000×2250 test image, resized it to the constrained sizes above, and encoded it as JPEG at six quality settings in Chrome:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Output size&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;q .95&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;q .90&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;q .80&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;q .70&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;q .50&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;q .30&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;200×200&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8.4 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5.9 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.3 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3.8 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3.0 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2.4 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;750×352&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;29.0 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;18.4 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12.8 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10.4 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;6.6 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5.4 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;800×800&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;56.7 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;33.6 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20.9 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15.5 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11.7 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;9.5 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;750×1000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;64.3 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;38.5 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;24.0 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;18.2 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;13.5 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11.0 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Read the caveat before you reuse these numbers&lt;/strong&gt;: my test image is synthetic colour gradients, which compress &lt;em&gt;much&lt;/em&gt; better than a photograph. Fabric weave, wood grain, food texture, printed text — the things product photos are made of — cost several times more bytes at the same size and quality. So treat this table as a &lt;em&gt;floor&lt;/em&gt;, not an estimate for your catalogue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That reframes the 32 KB slot honestly: at 200×200 even quality 0.95 produced 8.4 KB here, so the ceiling is not absurd on its own. What makes it dangerous is that the same quality setting on a real, textured photo can be an order of magnitude larger — and you only find out at upload time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why "export at quality 80" doesn't solve this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;File size is roughly &lt;code&gt;pixels × bits-per-pixel&lt;/code&gt;. Resizing changes the first term by the square; quality changes the second by &lt;strong&gt;3–6×&lt;/strong&gt; across the whole usable range (measured above: 56.7 KB → 9.5 KB from q .95 to q .30 at 800×800). So:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Going 4000×3000 → 800×800 cuts pixel count by &lt;strong&gt;~19×&lt;/strong&gt;. That is the big lever.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Going quality 0.95 → 0.50 saves roughly &lt;strong&gt;3–5×&lt;/strong&gt;, and starts to be visible on product edges and fine text.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you pick one quality for everything, you are choosing the second lever only, and you will either ship 3 MB files that fail the 100 KB slot, or crush every image to satisfy the single tightest ceiling you happen to have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What actually fits: downscale first, then search the quality
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The honest algorithm is two steps, and the second one is a search, not a guess:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resize so the image is exactly the required pixel size (never rely on the file size to imply dimensions).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If a ceiling exists, &lt;strong&gt;binary-search the encoder quality&lt;/strong&gt; until the encoded bytes fit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is what my own pipeline does, and it is not clever — it is just measurement. The search runs between quality 0.30 and 0.95 with at most 8 encode attempts, taking the highest quality that fits:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// canvas -&amp;gt; Blob, then binary search on quality for a byte budget&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeWithBudget&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;maxBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;hi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.95&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;best&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;hi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;           &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// encode(q) -&amp;gt; Promise&amp;lt;Blob&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;maxBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;best&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;blob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;hi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;hi&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.02&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;break&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;best&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                              &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// null =&amp;gt; even q=0.30 doesn't fit&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two properties matter more than the exact numbers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It returns &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt; instead of lying.&lt;/strong&gt; If quality 0.30 still exceeds the ceiling, you must shrink the image or change the format — silently shipping an oversized file just moves the rejection downstream.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It never re-encodes more than ~8 times per image.&lt;/strong&gt; With 51 outputs per photo you can feel an unbounded search loop on a phone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For reference, an audit run of 15 outputs from my tool (200×200 up to 1000×2000, white-background and transparent slots mixed) produced JPEGs between &lt;strong&gt;15 KB and 44 KB&lt;/strong&gt;. That is under every ceiling in the table except the 32 KB thumbnail — and 32 KB sits &lt;em&gt;inside&lt;/em&gt; that range, so whether an output passes depends on the image, which is the whole point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A five-second check on your own folder
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before blaming the platform, see which files are over which ceiling. macOS / Linux:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;find ./exports &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-iname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'*.jpg'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-size&lt;/span&gt; +2048k &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-exec&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;ls&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-lh&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Windows PowerShell:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight powershell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Get-ChildItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;\exports\&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;jpg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Where-Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-gt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;2MB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Select-Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And if you want to know what a given pixel size can reach at all, measure the encoder instead of trusting a blog post:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// returns the smallest quality that fits, or null&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;probe&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;img&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;maxBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OffscreenCanvas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;2d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;drawImage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;img&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodeWithBudget&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;convertToBlob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;image/jpeg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;quality&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;maxBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


The part nobody documents
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 38 rules without a published ceiling are not permission to upload 40 MB. In practice they mean &lt;em&gt;"the platform will re-encode your file and may reject it if it is absurd"&lt;/em&gt;. I kept them as &lt;code&gt;maxKB: null&lt;/code&gt; in the dataset rather than inventing a number, because a made-up limit is worse than a missing one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Same for the white-background slots: &lt;strong&gt;16 of the 51 rules&lt;/strong&gt; require a white background, and "white" means the pixels, not the intent. In my own test images the "white" sweep of a photo-style shot measured &lt;strong&gt;246–252&lt;/strong&gt; on all three channels — visibly white on screen, and not white to a checker that wants 255. If your pipeline only fills the letterbox padding with white, you will fail these slots even though the image "looks white".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where the data lives
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full set — 18 platforms, 51 rules, a &lt;code&gt;maxKB&lt;/code&gt; (or explicit &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt;) and a &lt;code&gt;sourceUrl&lt;/code&gt; on every rule:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;JSON: &lt;code&gt;https://tugui.top/data/specs.json&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CSV: &lt;code&gt;https://tugui.top/data/specs.csv&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;npm: &lt;code&gt;npm install tugui-specs&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;mirror: &lt;a href="https://github.com/pgodace/tugui-specs-data" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/pgodace/tugui-specs-data&lt;/a&gt;, MIT&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;25 of the 51 rules trace to the platform's own documentation, 26 only to third-party write-ups — each rule says which. If a ceiling has changed, send me the URL and I will fix it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I also build the tool that produced the measurements in this post: it resizes one photo into every marketplace size in the browser, nothing uploaded (&lt;a href="https://tugui.top" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tugui.top&lt;/a&gt;). Five files per batch free, $4/month or $12/year. The dataset is useful without it.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This week delivered two of the more practically meaningful cost reductions in recent memory—GPT-6.1 Terra cuts vision-reasoning spend in half with no migration pain, and the Decisions API finally makes structured classification a first-class API primitive. Layered on top: voice generation that actually scales, multimodal search without caption hacks, and a Rust TypeScript compiler that's either a curiosity or a lifeline depending on how bad your type-check latency is.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


GPT-6.1 Terra cuts vision-reasoning costs by half
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GPT-6.1 Terra ships at $1/M input tokens—50% cheaper than GPT-5.6 Terra—with a reported 15-point benchmark improvement on agent tasks. The migration story is as frictionless as it gets: swap &lt;code&gt;gpt-5.6-terra&lt;/code&gt; for &lt;code&gt;gpt-6.1-terra&lt;/code&gt; in your model ID, no prompt changes required.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical unlock here is high-volume document automation. Invoice processing, form extraction, technical drawing interpretation—these workloads have historically been expensive enough to force architectural compromises (batching, aggressive caching, selective routing). At half the cost with better accuracy, you can straighten those pipelines out and run them more aggressively.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The routing heuristic is simple: GPT-6.1 Terra for everything that was running on GPT-5.6 Terra, GPT-6 Astra only when you genuinely need maximum-difficulty reasoning and can justify the cost delta.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Verdict: Ship.&lt;/strong&gt; Zero-friction replacement with meaningful cost and accuracy gains. Deploy today for any cost-sensitive vision or agentic workload.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Decisions API reaches GA with 10x latency gains
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Decisions API is a bounded-answer classification endpoint built on GPT-6 Luna. It trades generative flexibility for speed and cost on discrete decisions—routing logic, classification gates, scoring—and now handles 400 questions per request at $0.10/M input tokens ($0.01/M cached).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The problem it solves is concrete: agentic workflows are full of sequential completion calls that exist solely to make structured yes/no or categorical decisions. Each call carries the full overhead of a generative endpoint even when you only need a label. The Decisions API collapses those chains into a single request with a defined schema (predicate, choice, or score types) and returns results at 10x lower latency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Native GPT-Live integration is the other notable addition—real-time voice and multimodal decision loops without the context marshaling overhead that makes current implementations painful to maintain. Regional residency (US/EU) and prompt caching are included at GA.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The upfront cost is schema definition. You need to think through your decision types before you can use this, which means it rewards teams who've already mapped their classification logic. If your app makes the same routing or gating decision hundreds of times per day, the pricing model heavily favors you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Verdict: Ship&lt;/strong&gt; for any recurring classification or routing decision. Start with one high-frequency decision, validate schema design, then expand.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Gemini 3.8 Flash TTS scales voice generation infinitely
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google's Flash TTS lets you generate custom character voices from natural language prompts, replicate voices from 30-second audio samples, and control performance at the line level—tone, pacing, dialect, speaker isolation. SynthID watermarking and consent verification are built into the replication flow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The meaningful shift is moving from preset voice libraries to programmatic voice design. If you're building audio agents, multilingual content pipelines, or interactive media, you've been either picking from a fixed palette or routing around TTS limitations with external tooling. This replaces both approaches with an API-driven model that scales without per-voice licensing overhead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consent verification for voice replication (30-second sample plus verbal consent recording) is a real workflow requirement, not optional. Plan for it in your data collection pipeline if replication is part of your use case. Flash-Lite TTS targets high-volume dubbing at lower cost for scenarios where dynamic voice design per request isn't required.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Verdict: Evaluate.&lt;/strong&gt; Available now in Google AI Studio. Worth running through the playground against your current voice solution before committing to integration work.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Cloudflare AI Search adds multimodal embeddings, OCR
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cloudflare AI Search now embeds images directly using Qwen3-VL-Embedding alongside text, handles 10 MiB PDFs with built-in OCR, and bills per query rather than by capacity. Free tier covers 5M ingestion tokens and 1,000 semantic queries per month; billing starts November 1, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The caption-generation workaround for image search has always been lossy—you embed a description of an image rather than the image itself, and visual detail that doesn't survive captioning disappears from your index. Native multimodal embeddings fix the retrieval quality problem at the source. OCR on ingest removes the preprocessing step that makes PDF indexing tedious to operationalize.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Setup requires Workers AI configuration and a choice between text-only and multimodal embedding models. If your index is text-only, the multimodal model adds overhead you don't need. If you're indexing product catalogs, screenshots, or scanned documents, it's the right default.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Verdict: Evaluate&lt;/strong&gt; if you're running caption-based image search or preprocessing PDFs manually. The free tier makes it low-risk to index a representative sample and compare retrieval quality directly.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Ollama defaults models to MLX on Apple Silicon
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ollama v0.40.0-rc3 automatically routes supported model architectures (Qwen, Gemma, and others) to the MLX runtime on Apple Silicon, eliminating the manual backend selection that previously required explicit configuration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For macOS developers running local LLMs, this removes the gap between "installed" and "actually fast." CPU fallback was the silent performance killer for anyone who hadn't manually configured MLX—now native acceleration is the default and opt-out is the override.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Architecture support is the constraint. Check your model against the supported list before assuming MLX acceleration applies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Verdict: Ship&lt;/strong&gt; if you're on Apple Silicon and v0.40.0-rc3+. Upgrade, verify your models are in the supported set, and move on.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Cloudflare ports TypeScript to Rust, ships experimental build
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;tsrs&lt;/code&gt; is a Rust implementation of TypeScript that runs 8x faster than &lt;code&gt;tsc&lt;/code&gt; with 70% lower memory usage and compiles to WebAssembly. It's installable today via npm as an experimental package.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The WebAssembly target is the more interesting technical story. TypeScript checking in browser and edge environments—without Node.js overhead—opens deployment topologies for build systems and developer tooling that weren't practical before. The raw speed improvement matters for large monorepos where type-check latency is a genuine iteration bottleneck.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Diagnostic output ordering differences from &lt;code&gt;tsc&lt;/code&gt; mean you should test against your existing codebase before relying on it in CI. Stable release is still pending.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Verdict: Evaluate&lt;/strong&gt; if type-check latency is actively slowing your team or you're targeting Wasm deployments. Too early to swap into production CI without validation work.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;If this kind of weekly signal-to-noise filtering is useful for your work, Signal goes out every week at &lt;a href="https://repowrapped.com/signal" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;repowrapped.com/signal&lt;/a&gt;. Senior engineers who don't have time to chase every announcement tend to find it worth the inbox slot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two weeks ago we told you a Think deeper message in Rovo costs 65 to 86 credits. That was wrong. We measured two runs on one evening and wrote the range up as if it meant something. When we exported our org's usage five days later, it had single chat messages at 203, 238, 268 and 310 credits, and nobody went back to check the article against it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then an admin at another company told us they're seeing single calls above 300. So on 27 October we went back to our test org (a pool of 3,920 credits this period) and sent 21 more messages, one at a time, reading the meter after each. They cost 1,285 credits. Think deeper came in anywhere between 10 and 182. The same prompt, word for word, cost 12 on one run and 134 on another.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's a small sample on a small org, and I'll say where it runs out. If you put our 65 to 86 into a budget, take it out. The &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/blog/rovo-credits-per-user?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;original article&lt;/a&gt; stays up as it was.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What our usage export says
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Atlassian Administration will email you a CSV with one row per billable Rovo event (Gabriela walked through the export in &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/tutorials/check-rovo-credits-usage-each-user?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;how to check Rovo credits usage for each user&lt;/a&gt;). Ours was exported on 20 October 2026 at 21:46 UTC and has eleven columns: Usage Id, Usage Count, Date, Org ID, Site ID, Site Name, App, Actor ID, Feature, Agent, Feature Status. There's no column for the mode.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It has 19 Rovo Chat rows between 8 September and 20 October. Sorted by credits: 10 eleven times, then 65, 77, 78, 86, 203, 238, 268, 310.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F6c9f132d4fac494d510b3f44937b4c105dab8a46-1800x945.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F6c9f132d4fac494d510b3f44937b4c105dab8a46-1800x945.png" title="Our export's 19 Rovo Chat messages, plotted from the CSV. The four bars on the right add up to 1,019 of the 1,435 chat credits." alt="Bar chart of all 19 Rovo Chat messages in our usage export from 8 September to 20 October 2026: eleven at 10 credits, then 65, 77, 78 and 86, then 203, 238, 268 and 310. A dotted line marks 86, the top of the range our 15 Oct article reported, and a dashed line marks 200, the top of Atlassian's light task band" width="800" height="420"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three of the big ones came within twelve minutes of each other on the morning of 11 September (310, 268, 238). On 14 October at 07:56 UTC came the 203, the same day we measured. That evening also had two messages at 77 and 78 that never made our table. I don't know what those prompts were. Nobody wrote them down, and I'm not going to guess.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We picked two cheap runs and didn't look at our own data afterwards. That's on me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Rovo credits per prompt: the same prompt cost 12, 49, 65 and 134
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We started by repeating our own 15 October prompt, the one that cost 86: "Our 40-person company runs Jira Standard and Confluence Standard. Draft a one-page plan for rolling out a change advisory process: roles, approval steps, and what to measure in the first month."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We forced Think deeper from the mode menu on each run. Four runs: 134, 49, 65, 12.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Look at the 12. Rovo's step panel on that run says "This is a writing task, so I don't need heavy tool use", and it finished in 2 steps. On the 65 run it ran a Teamwork Graph search first. For the 134 and the 49 we didn't capture the steps, so I can't tell you what Rovo did on those.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F465b3b1ab0c58b8cd1cabdc59b3f7a4a4b574d11-1440x1000.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F465b3b1ab0c58b8cd1cabdc59b3f7a4a4b574d11-1440x1000.png" title="The 12-credit run of the change advisory prompt. Notice Completed 2 steps and the line about not needing heavy tool use. The same prompt cost 134 on another run." alt="Rovo Chat answering the change advisory prompt in Think deeper mode: Completed 2 steps, with the step text This is a writing task, so I don't need heavy tool use, followed by the start of a plan titled Change Advisory Process: One-Page Rollout Plan" width="800" height="556"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What moves Think deeper credits from 10 to 182
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are the 21 messages, all on our wolfaenpak test org. The meter never moved between messages, so nothing else was spending, and each message's full charge was already there at the first read, 21 to 22 seconds after the answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What we asked&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Mode&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Read site data?&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Credits&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Change advisory plan, four runs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;not captured, not captured, Teamwork Graph search, no&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;134, 49, 65, 12&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same plan as a thread, follow-ups 2 to 4&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;no&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;44, 29, 26&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Summarise a 100-word incident report&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;no&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Summarise a 2,000-word incident report, twice&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;no&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;23, 26&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Write a 1,500-word rollout plan, twice&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;no&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;16, 15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Summarise every page in a 13-page Confluence space, twice&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;57, 80&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Group all 469 open items in a Jira project, three times&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;106, 182, 141&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Follow-ups on that Jira thread&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;86, 79&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Find payment and login failures across every Jira project&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Think deeper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;94&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The 469-item Jira question again&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Let Rovo decide&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Think deeper, every run we could inspect fits one rule: the price followed whether Rovo went off to read Jira or Confluence. Runs where it read nothing cost 10 to 26 as fresh messages. Runs where we saw it read site data cost 57 to 182.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I thought length would drive it. Pasting 2,000 words instead of 100 added about 15 credits. Asking for a 1,500-word plan got answers of about 2,000 words for 15 and 16 credits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Steps aren't the meter either. The run with 75 steps cost 79, the one with 54 cost 94, and the one with 27 cost 182. On the 182 run Rovo did 5 Jira searches over 469 open items and named 33 of them in its answer. My guess is the charge follows how much text goes through the model while it reasons, retrieved data included. I can't prove it, and Atlassian doesn't publish a formula.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2Ff06182b30fd7b558bde4c97092c8720df3c97089-1440x1000.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2Ff06182b30fd7b558bde4c97092c8720df3c97089-1440x1000.png" title="The 182-credit message. Notice the repeated Searching Jira issues steps: Rovo deciding to read the whole project." alt="Rovo Chat in Think deeper mode on the question Look at all open work items in the Jira project SUPPORT, with the step panel open: Completed 27 steps, then Finding the right tools, Analyzing, Read Jira service guidance, Search all open work items in Jira project SUPPORT, three Searching Jira issues compactly steps, and an Analyzing note that there are 469 open issues" width="800" height="556"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our plain thread went 49, 44, 29, 26, and the Jira thread went 141, 86, 79, so later messages didn't get dearer. Follow-ups did cost a bit more than a fresh short prompt (26 to 44 against 10 to 16), which would fit a small charge for the context the thread carries. Three data points, so treat it as a hint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then the row that annoyed me most. On Let Rovo decide, the 469-item question cost 11 credits. That answer says it reviewed all 469 items, found the same six themes, counted the same 166 overdue items and named 26 work items. Its step panel says Completed 24 steps. On Think deeper the same question cost 106, 141 and 182. The work looked the same from where I sat. It's one run, so I won't call it a rule, but on that question the mode label was the difference between 11 and 182.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F4f82eba9b6d2aad61e38f57efb9a398855aad50a-1600x896.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F4f82eba9b6d2aad61e38f57efb9a398855aad50a-1600x896.png" title="Illustration, generated locally with ERNIE-Image. One coffee, and the waiter read the whole filing cabinet before bringing it. The bill comes after the coffee." alt="Cartoon of a stunned blue robot at a diner table holding a receipt for one coffee that runs across the floor, while a dusty silver robot waiter in a bow tie comes back from a back room of open filing cabinets" width="800" height="448"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Rovo credits pricing: Atlassian's own bands already allowed 300
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Atlassian's pages allowed for all of this. We misread them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://support.atlassian.com/rovo/docs/rovo-usage-limits/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;How Rovo credits work&lt;/a&gt; page lists Think deeper under "Variable credits per billable event" and names four things that move the number: "Reasoning steps", "Search volume", "Tools invoked" and "Model power". It also says Think deeper "dynamically plans, searches, reasons, and iterates to return a thorough answer, with built-in quality validation that automatically retries if it doesn't meet the quality bar." Atlassian's &lt;a href="https://www.atlassian.com/resources/usage" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;usage pricing page&lt;/a&gt; adds "the output generated", which our long-answer runs didn't bear out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That support page also links an image called "Rovo Credits: Task Types". Light tasks are "Estimated Rovo Credits 10-200". Medium tasks are "201-500". Heavy tasks are "500+". The Wayback Machine's capture of 14 September doesn't have the image, and its capture of 17 September does. We quoted those bands in the first article and also wrote that Atlassian "publishes no number" for Think deeper. The second statement was wrong, and I should have caught it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So our 310 sits in Atlassian's own medium band, and a 300-plus call like the one we were told about is at least medium. The card has a heavy band above 500.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rovo "will activate think deeper mode automatically if it detects a multi-layered or open-ended prompt", says the &lt;a href="https://support.atlassian.com/rovo/docs/compare-rovo-chat-reasoning-modes/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;reasoning modes page&lt;/a&gt;. Your users don't have to pick the expensive mode to get it. And Rovo shows no estimate before you send. &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-866" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-866&lt;/a&gt;, "Show estimated credit cost before submitting a prompt", had 6 votes on 27 October, and its description still quotes June's flat 10 and 100.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why this hurts on 3 December
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From 3 December, extra usage is billed at "$0.01 per Rovo credit, or $10 per 1,000 credits", and "extra usage is on (by default)". A Jira Standard seat gets 25 credits a month. So our 182-credit Jira question is more than seven seat-months, and the 310 is twelve, or $3.10.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your pool is shared, and you can't cap a person yet. The support page says "Coming soon: you'll be able to allocate Rovo credits by user", with no date. On &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-1224" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-1224&lt;/a&gt; a customer wrote on 26 October that one user spent 101,000 of their org's 196,000 credits in a month, and added: "Hearing there is no timeline for said feature feels nothing more like a cash grab." The broader request, &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-532" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-532&lt;/a&gt;, had 57 votes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can't switch Think deeper off for Chat either. &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-1302" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-1302&lt;/a&gt; asks for control over the automatic switch (2 votes), and &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-1317" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-1317&lt;/a&gt; asks for an admin setting for the default mode (12 votes). And a usage limit won't stop an expensive message halfway: when a limit is hit, Atlassian says work already running completes and only new credit-consuming requests pause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Afterwards you can't see which messages were the expensive kind. Our Features tab for the billing period that started 15 October, test included, shows "Rovo Chat 1,438 credits", every mode on one line. That's a different window from the export's 1,435, so the near match is a coincidence. The Teamwork Graph line stayed at 0 even though Rovo ran Teamwork Graph searches inside our chats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2Fedc14588167f7df56a20c7478037e0aff742dc7f-1440x1100.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2Fedc14588167f7df56a20c7478037e0aff742dc7f-1440x1100.png" title="Our Features tab for the billing period that started 15 October, after the test. Chat messages sit on one Rovo Chat line whatever the mode." alt="Atlassian Administration, Meters, Rovo credits, Features tab for Oct 15 to Nov 15 2026 on our test org: Rovo Chat 1,438 credits, AI Slides 364, Rovo Agents 196, Self Service Agent 150, Teamwork Graph 0" width="800" height="611"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No quote before you send, no mode in the export, no cap per person. I can't budget that, and I doubt you can.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F71657b5341739a16f3e64759823f94769a049228-1600x896.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F71657b5341739a16f3e64759823f94769a049228-1600x896.png" title="Illustration, generated locally with ERNIE-Image. The driver took the long way through your archives, and the receipt shows the total without the route." alt="Cartoon of a worried silver robot passenger clutching a wallet in the back of a yellow taxi while a grinning green robot driver steers through a maze of library shelves and the meter throws sparks" width="" height=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I'd do as an admin this week
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Export your CSV now and sort the Rovo Chat rows by Usage Count. If your top rows are in the hundreds, that's your real per-message price. The &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/tutorials/check-rovo-credits-usage-each-user?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;export walkthrough&lt;/a&gt; shows where the button is, and the emailed link expires after 24 hours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tell people which questions cost money. Asking Rovo to read a project or a space ("look at all open items", "summarise every page") was our expensive class. Writing and rewriting stayed cheap even in Think deeper.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Try Let Rovo decide on the big read-everything questions before Think deeper. In our one run on the 469-item question it found the same themes and overdue count for 11 credits. One run proves little, but it's a cheap thing to test on your own org.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set a usage limit before 3 December, or decide not to on purpose. The &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/tutorials/rovo-usage-limit-extra-usage?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;usage limit walkthrough&lt;/a&gt; covers both settings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Re-check any budget built on a fixed Think deeper price. Ours modelled it at 75.5 credits in the &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/blog/atlassian-usage-based-pricing-budget-2027?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;usage-based pricing budget&lt;/a&gt;, which is too low for data questions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some of this we couldn't test. Deep Research has disappeared from the mode menu on our org, and the chat composer has no agent or model picker, so nothing here covers either. Our biggest Jira project has 469 open items and our biggest Confluence space has 13 pages. Yours are probably bigger, and my guess is that's how people get past 300.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What's the most expensive single Rovo message in your export? If you've got one above 310, I'd like to know what the question was.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Originally published on &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/blog/rovo-credits-cost-think-deeper?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;leanzero.net&lt;/a&gt;. More Atlassian, Forge and local-AI write-ups at &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/blog?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;leanzero.net/blog&lt;/a&gt;, and if you're planning a migration or a Forge app, that's what we do: &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/services?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;leanzero.net/services&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Build a Custom CMS?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hi, I am Kaleb. I run Dream Futures Tech out of Pompano Beach, Florida. When I was rebuilding my own business website, I needed a straightforward blog publishing workflow. I did not want a heavy headless CMS subscription or a bloated monolith database setup. I already run a small automation server at home, and I host my main site on standard infrastructure. What I wanted was simple: write drafts, hit publish, and have the static pages update instantly without rebuilding the entire site from scratch every time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To solve this, I built a lightweight blog content management system directly into my Next.js 14 application using Prisma and SQLite. It keeps things contained, fast, and entirely under my control. In my judgment, writing your own lightweight CMS for a simple business blog takes less time than configuring a third-party service once you factor in authentication and API keys.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Prisma Schema for Posts and Drafts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything starts with the database schema. I use Prisma because the type safety prevents entire classes of silly bugs when moving data from form inputs to the database. Here is the core model I use for my blog posts:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;model Post {
id        String   @id @default(cuid())
title     String
slug      String   @unique
content   String
published Boolean  @default(false)
createdAt DateTime @default(now())
updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt
}
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;published&lt;/code&gt; boolean is the gatekeeper. When I query the database for public-facing routes, I strictly filter by &lt;code&gt;published: true&lt;/code&gt;. In the admin dashboard route, I fetch all records so I can view, edit, and delete my working drafts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Handling Drafts in the UI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Separating drafts from published posts on the frontend is straightforward with Next.js App Router. My public route uses a simple Prisma query:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/blog/page.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/lib/db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;BlogIndex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;findMany&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;published&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;orderBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;createdAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;desc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;article&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;slug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/article&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="p"&gt;))}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For the admin side, I built a protected route that lists both drafts and published entries, allowing me to iterate on formatting and copy before pushing it live to the world.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Incremental Static Regeneration and RevalidatePath
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next.js 14 provides powerful caching mechanisms, but stale static pages can be frustrating when you are trying to publish a new article. I wanted the performance of static generation combined with instant updates upon publishing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I used Incremental Static Regeneration (ISR) combined with Next.js Server Actions to handle this. When I update a post's status to published in the admin panel, the Server Action updates the database and immediately calls &lt;code&gt;revalidatePath&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is what that Server Action looks like in practice:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/admin/actions.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;use server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/lib/db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;revalidatePath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;next/cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;togglePublish&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;currentStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;published&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;currentStatus&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Invalidate the public blog index and the specific post route&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;revalidatePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/blog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;revalidatePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`/blog/[slug]`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When &lt;code&gt;togglePublish&lt;/code&gt; runs, Next.js clears the cache for those specific paths in the background. The next time a visitor requests the blog index or the specific article, they get the freshly generated static page without waiting for a full manual deployment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Keeping It Maintainable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building this internal tool directly into my Next.js codebase saved me from managing external API tokens, webhooks, and third-party dashboard limits. SQLite handles the data cleanly on my hosting provider, and the codebase remains small enough that I can audit every line myself. If you are managing a small business site and want total control over your publishing pipeline without adding unnecessary infrastructure overhead, combining Next.js App Router, Prisma, and &lt;code&gt;revalidatePath&lt;/code&gt; is a reliable path forward.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build sites and automations for small businesses at dreamfuturestech.com.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built WiseTube because the YouTube sidebar kept leading my kids somewhere I didn't want them. It's a free library of hand-picked learning videos that play inside my own player, with YouTube's suggestions, Shorts and autoplay-to-the-next-thing taken out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The covering-up part gets most of the attention. This post is about the smaller stuff that cost me evenings. If you're wrapping the IFrame API for anything, some of these will probably find you too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5uhlmhnfrxxiw2d6ly1w.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5uhlmhnfrxxiw2d6ly1w.png" alt="The phone view, where a kid actually taps all this." width="780" height="1688"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. The API script can hang, so give it a deadline
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You load &lt;code&gt;https://www.youtube.com/iframe_api&lt;/code&gt; and wait for &lt;code&gt;onYouTubeIframeAPIReady&lt;/code&gt;. Most of the time it's fine. On a flaky connection it can sit there forever, and a kid staring at a spinner will go find another app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wrap the load in a promise, cache it so it only happens once, and clear the cache if the script errors so the next try starts clean. The player also starts a 15 second timer. If &lt;code&gt;onReady&lt;/code&gt; hasn't fired by then, it shows "The film didn't load" with a retry button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. YouTube resets your playback speed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I save speed and volume to localStorage so a kid who likes 1.25x doesn't have to set it every time. I set the rate in &lt;code&gt;onReady&lt;/code&gt;, and that isn't enough, because YouTube can reset the rate when a film starts playing. The fix is boring. On every PLAYING state change, read &lt;code&gt;getPlaybackRate()&lt;/code&gt; and set it again if it doesn't match the saved value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Autoplay for the chosen film still gets blocked sometimes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I want the film a kid picked to start right away, so the player is created with &lt;code&gt;autoplay: 1&lt;/code&gt;. Browsers don't always allow it. There's no clean event for "autoplay was blocked," so after &lt;code&gt;onReady&lt;/code&gt; I wait about 1.2 seconds and check the state. If it's still UNSTARTED or CUED, I show my own big play button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the only autoplay in the app. When a film ends, nothing else starts on its own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Don't let callbacks restart the player
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Player component takes &lt;code&gt;onTick&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onEnded&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;onError&lt;/code&gt; from the page. If those are effect dependencies, any parent re-render can tear down the iframe and build a new one mid-film. I use React's &lt;code&gt;useEffectEvent&lt;/code&gt; for them, so the effect that mounts the player only depends on the video id, the retry count and the title.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Progress gets read every 250ms for the scrub bar but only reported up every 5 seconds. Saving progress four times a second felt silly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Validate pasted links before you trust them
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Parents can add a film by pasting a YouTube link. People paste everything: &lt;code&gt;youtu.be&lt;/code&gt; links, &lt;code&gt;/embed/&lt;/code&gt; links, &lt;code&gt;watch?v=&lt;/code&gt; links, Shorts. I parse the URL, accept the normal shapes, and reject anything under &lt;code&gt;/shorts/&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;/live/&lt;/code&gt; with "Paste a normal YouTube link. Shorts stay out of WiseTube."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I call YouTube's oEmbed endpoint for the link. If it doesn't come back with a title and channel name, the film doesn't get added. That catches typos, private videos and videos that can't be embedded, before a kid ever taps them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. A PIN on a device with no server
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are no accounts. Profiles, progress and settings sit in localStorage. The four-digit parent PIN is stored as a SHA-256 hash with a random 16-byte salt, using &lt;code&gt;crypto.subtle&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'll be straight about what that buys. A four-digit PIN on a device the kid holds is a speed bump. It keeps a six-year-old out of the settings. A teenager with dev tools could get past it. For a free app with no backend, I'm fine with that, and I'd rather say it than pretend it's a vault.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because everything is on one device, the parent settings have an export button that downloads a &lt;code&gt;wisetube-library.json&lt;/code&gt; with profiles, PIN, added and hidden films, and progress. Import shows how many learners are in the file and asks before replacing anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Febc6qncawrkvx6xb9516.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Febc6qncawrkvx6xb9516.png" alt="The little-kids look. The PIN keeps them in this lane." width="780" height="1688"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. Vite preview lies about routing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The site is a static build on Cloudflare Pages. Public pages are prerendered for search. Private app routes like &lt;code&gt;/watch/:youtubeId&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;/parent&lt;/code&gt; are rewritten to a noindex &lt;code&gt;app.html&lt;/code&gt; through &lt;code&gt;public/_redirects&lt;/code&gt;, so a refresh doesn't 404.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;vite preview&lt;/code&gt; ignores &lt;code&gt;_redirects&lt;/code&gt; and Pages' 404 behavior, so a route can look fine locally and behave differently once deployed. I test routing with &lt;code&gt;npx wrangler pages dev dist&lt;/code&gt;, which behaves like the real thing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


8. Tests for the catalog, too
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The video list is data, and data drifts. &lt;code&gt;npm run check&lt;/code&gt; runs TypeScript, oxlint and vitest, and the catalog tests fail if an import sneaks in a Short, a duplicate, an unknown topic, or a channel I already ruled out. That has caught more mistakes than any of my UI tests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can try the result at &lt;a href="https://wisetube.yuriystasyuk.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;wisetube.yuriystasyuk.com&lt;/a&gt;. If you've wrapped the IFrame API and found a cleaner way around any of these, I'd love to hear it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


When Your Settings Modal Looks Perfect But Traps Focus Behind Itself
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You ship a settings panel. Design sign-off was clean. Two weeks later, a customer using VoiceOver reports they cannot rename their workspace because focus jumps to the page footer mid-dialog. You add &lt;code&gt;tabindex="-1"&lt;/code&gt; to the overlay. Another customer on Windows Narrator complains the Escape key closes the browser tab instead of the modal. You patch keyboard listeners. Six months in, you maintain 140 lines of focus management that breaks whenever someone nests a dropdown inside the dialog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The expensive defect was not the border radius. It was treating modal semantics as a styling problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I evaluated &lt;a href="https://github.com/Nutlope/hallmark" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Nutlope/hallmark&lt;/a&gt; as design guidance, not as the owner of interaction contracts. Its anti-slop focus addresses visual sameness in AI-generated interfaces. The integration boundary matters: let design direction shape hierarchy and composition; application code must enforce semantics, state transitions, and motion constraints.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an architectural adoption pattern based on that stated scope, not a claim that I audited the repository's implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The boundary: design intent outside, interaction guarantees inside
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a reusable rename dialog, I separate three responsibilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application owns the saved name and persistence. The component owns the temporary draft and submission boundary. Radix Dialog owns modal behavior: focus containment, Escape dismissal, accessible labeling, and focus restoration under its normal lifecycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tailwind handles visible states. A small CSS layer handles responsive action layout and entrance motion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That separation prevents a common production failure: a visually customized modal gradually replacing accessibility behavior with document listeners and hand-maintained lists of focusable elements. Those lists break when validation inserts another control, a button becomes disabled, or nested content changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The implementation below assumes a working React/TypeScript application with Tailwind CSS v4. Install &lt;code&gt;@radix-ui/react-dialog&lt;/code&gt;; for Tailwind's Vite integration, install &lt;code&gt;tailwindcss&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;@tailwindcss/vite&lt;/code&gt;, then register &lt;code&gt;tailwindcss()&lt;/code&gt; alongside the React plugin in the existing Vite configuration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A dialog with a narrow contract
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This component deliberately accepts a synchronous &lt;code&gt;onSave&lt;/code&gt;. It commits a valid draft and closes. Network persistence needs a separate pending-and-error contract; pretending every save completes instantly is another tutorial shortcut that leaks into production.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;as &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@radix-ui/react-dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RenameDialogProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;rounded-md px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;focus-visible:outline-indigo-600&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;RenameDialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RenameDialogProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setDraft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fieldId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Root&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onOpenChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setDraft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Trigger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;asChild&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; border border-slate-300`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
Rename workspace
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Trigger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Portal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Overlay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"fixed inset-0 z-40 bg-slate-950/50"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Content&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;rename-dialog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fixed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;top-1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;left-1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;z-50&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;max-h-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;calc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;100dvh_-_2rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;)]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;w-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;calc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;_-_2rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;)]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;max-w-lg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="err"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;translate-x-1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="err"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;translate-y-1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;overflow-y-auto&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;rounded-xl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;border&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;border-slate-200&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;bg-white&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;p-6&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;text-slate-950&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;shadow-xl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Title&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
Rename workspace
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Description&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
Choose a name your teammates will recognize.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"mt-6"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;preventDefault&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;htmlFor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fieldId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"text-sm font-medium"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
Workspace name
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fieldId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onChange&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setDraft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;required&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;maxLength&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;80&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;autoComplete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"off"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;mt-2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;block&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;w-full&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;rounded-md&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;border&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;border-slate-400&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;px-3&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;py-2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;focus-visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;outline-2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;focus-visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;outline-indigo-600&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"rename-actions mt-6"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;asChild&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"button"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; border border-slate-300`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
Cancel
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"submit"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;disabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; bg-indigo-700 text-white
disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-50`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
Save name
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Portal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Radix supplies &lt;code&gt;role="dialog"&lt;/code&gt;, modal semantics, and the title/description associations. Adding duplicate ARIA attributes manually would create another source of truth. The input keeps an explicit label because dialog descriptions do not replace field labels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Motion should not become a layout engine
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A production failure pattern worth recognizing is a resize animation that reads &lt;code&gt;scrollHeight&lt;/code&gt;, writes &lt;code&gt;height&lt;/code&gt;, then measures again on every frame. Interleaving those reads and writes can force repeated layout work. Adding more animation frames does not fix the dependency cycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the complete global CSS configuration and micro-interaction layer:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;"tailwindcss"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.rename-dialog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;container-type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;inline-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.rename-dialog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;"open"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dialog-enter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;140ms&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;ease-out&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.rename-actions&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;display&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;flex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;flex-direction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;column&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;gap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.75rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@container&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;min-width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;24rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.rename-actions&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;flex-direction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;justify-content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;flex-end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@keyframes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dialog-enter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;scale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.98&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;scale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The animation runs once when &lt;code&gt;data-state="open"&lt;/code&gt; appears. No JavaScript intervention. Container queries handle button layout without breakpoints or ResizeObserver polling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When the contract changes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the save operation becomes async, this component's contract must expand. The natural boundary is to lift &lt;code&gt;draft&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;pending&lt;/code&gt; state out, then pass &lt;code&gt;onSave: (name: string) =&amp;gt; Promise&amp;lt;void&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; down. The component can then disable both buttons and the input while &lt;code&gt;pending&lt;/code&gt; is true, show inline error feedback, and only close on successful resolution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That separation keeps Radix Dialog responsible for focus and dismissal semantics, but lets the application control when closure is permitted. Disabling the close button during async work is a clear signal; silently ignoring Escape creates invisible modal behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The integration layer that matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A design system that delivers visual consistency does valuable work. The failure is assuming it also delivers interaction contracts. Focus traps, dismissal behavior, accessible labeling, and state coordination are not styling concerns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Radix Dialog provides those contracts. Tailwind provides utility composition. The boundary between them is load-bearing. Cross it intentionally or maintain the duplication yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvmr6izckjhbfhl0qgt5o.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvmr6izckjhbfhl0qgt5o.png" alt=" " width="800" height="451"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F37xn40swgfezmif24vdh.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F37xn40swgfezmif24vdh.jpg" alt=" " width="640" height="640"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Over the past few months, I have been working on &lt;strong&gt;NexoreUI v2.0&lt;/strong&gt; — a component library, CLI, and template studio built for &lt;strong&gt;Next.js 15, React 19, Tailwind CSS v4, Radix UI, and Framer Motion&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Live Documentation &amp;amp; Playground:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.nexoreui.site" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nexoreui.site&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Interactive Templates Studio:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.nexoreui.site/templates" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nexoreui.site/templates&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Visual Component Sandbox:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.nexoreui.site/nexoremake" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;nexoreui.site/nexoremake&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GitHub Repository (MIT):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/Al1mov77/NexoreUI" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/Al1mov77/NexoreUI&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a breakdown of why I built v2.0, the architecture decisions behind it, and what is included right now.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


1. The Problem: Why Modern UI Templates Started Looking Identical
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you browse recent SaaS landing pages or AI templates, you quickly notice the same patterns repeated everywhere:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Heavy purple/rainbow blur blobs and glowing borders on every card&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decorative pill badges above every single heading&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Animations that ignore &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt; and hurt performance&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hardcoded hex colors that break when switching between light and dark themes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With &lt;strong&gt;NexoreUI v2.0&lt;/strong&gt;, the goal was to build a system where hierarchy comes from &lt;strong&gt;typography, proportion, 1px borders, and semantic tokens&lt;/strong&gt; — so the interface remains clear even if you view it in grayscale, while still supporting rich interactive moments when needed.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


2. How the Core Library &amp;amp; CLI Work (MIT Open Source)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of locking you into an opaque dependency, NexoreUI supports two workflows:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;


Workflow A: Copy-Paste / CLI Source Ownership (Recommended)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can initialize &lt;code&gt;nexore.json&lt;/code&gt; in your project and pull the exact TypeScript component source into your &lt;code&gt;@/components/ui&lt;/code&gt; folder:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx nexoreui init
npx nexoreui add button card dialog tabs table
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Or scaffold a new Next.js / Vite + Tailwind CSS v4 project from scratch:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx nexoreui create my-app
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


Workflow B: Standard NPM Package
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you prefer importing components directly from &lt;code&gt;node_modules&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install &lt;/span&gt;nexoreui framer-motion lucide-react clsx tailwind-merge
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardHeader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardTitle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardDescription&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;nexoreui&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;nexoreui/styles.css&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ClusterStatusCard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"max-w-md"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardHeader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardTitle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Production Deployment&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardTitle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardDescription&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
Primary region nodes are synced and healthy.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardDescription&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardHeader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"flex items-center justify-between"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
12 / 12 nodes active
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"sm"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Inspect Logs&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CardContent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


What's in the Open-Source Core (60+ Components):
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Accessible Primitives:&lt;/strong&gt; Dialogs, Selects, Tabs, Accordions, Tooltips, Checkboxes, Switches, and Sliders built on Radix UI.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Data &amp;amp; Workflow UI:&lt;/strong&gt; Data Tables, Command Palettes, Steppers, File Uploads, Node Flow Graphs, and AI Prompt Inputs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tailwind CSS v4 Native:&lt;/strong&gt; Configured around semantic CSS variables (&lt;code&gt;--background&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;--foreground&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;--card&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;--border&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;--muted&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;--primary&lt;/code&gt;) and &lt;code&gt;@theme&lt;/code&gt; directives.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Accessible Motion:&lt;/strong&gt; Every Framer Motion animation checks &lt;code&gt;useReducedMotion()&lt;/code&gt; so users who prefer reduced motion see instant, static states without layout shift.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


3. Nexore Make: Visual Component Builder in the Browser
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes you don't want to tweak Tailwind classes blindly in your IDE. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inside the docs, I built &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.nexoreui.site/nexoremake" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Nexore Make&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; — a visual drag-and-drop sandbox where you can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reposition layers and adjust padding, border radius, shadows, and borders visually&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Preview changes in both Light and Dark mode on a blueprint grid&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Export clean &lt;strong&gt;TSX / JSX + Tailwind CSS&lt;/strong&gt; code directly into your clipboard&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


4. Production Templates: App UI &amp;amp; 240-Frame Canvas Scrubbing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.nexoreui.site/templates" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NexoreUI Templates&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, every template runs live in an interactive multi-device player (Desktop &lt;code&gt;1280px&lt;/code&gt;, Tablet &lt;code&gt;768px&lt;/code&gt;, Mobile &lt;code&gt;375px&lt;/code&gt;) with live theme switching.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each template in v2.0 is designed around an &lt;strong&gt;Identity Ledger&lt;/strong&gt; rule — meaning no two templates are allowed to share the same font pairing, surface treatment, layout archetype, and motion signature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They are split into two main engineering categories:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;App UI &amp;amp; CRUD Consoles:&lt;/strong&gt; Admin dashboards (&lt;code&gt;Nex Core Admin&lt;/code&gt;), high-density user tables with FLIP layout transitions, Swagger/OpenAPI form builders, auth flows, and storefronts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3D &amp;amp; HTML5 Canvas Scroll Scrubbers:&lt;/strong&gt; Product sheets and editorial showcases that scrub 240-frame video sequences on an HTML5 &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;canvas&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; using piecewise-linear scroll mapping and static poster fallbacks for &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


5. What is Free vs. What is Paid (Full Transparency)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I want to be 100% upfront about how the project is licensed:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Free &amp;amp; Open Source (MIT Forever):&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;All 60+ core UI components, motion helpers, and node graph components&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The CLI (&lt;code&gt;npx nexoreui init&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;npx nexoreui add&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;npx nexoreui create&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Full interactive browser previews of all templates + 5–10 code exports per session in the Nexore Make visual builder&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Optional Lifetime Pro ($39 one-time launch price, no subscription):&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Unlocks full standalone TSX source code and CLI scaffolding (&lt;code&gt;npx nexoreui add &amp;lt;template-slug&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, including automatic media/canvas asset downloads) for all 30+ flagship templates&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Unlimited code exports in Nexore Make&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Includes a workspace dashboard with &lt;strong&gt;2 permanent teammate invite links&lt;/strong&gt; so two other developers on your team get full Pro access at no extra cost&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Looking for Feedback
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are building with &lt;strong&gt;Next.js 15 / React 19 / Tailwind CSS v4&lt;/strong&gt;, I would love to hear your honest technical feedback:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How does the live multi-device preview in &lt;a href="https://www.nexoreui.site/templates" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;/templates&lt;/a&gt; perform on your machine?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Are there specific CRUD or data-heavy components you wish component libraries handled better out of the box?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thanks for reading and checking out the repo!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our internal dashboard kept its state in a big React context. It worked until it didn't: every refresh re-fetched everything, and the tabs lost their place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I moved the state into a small SQLite database in the browser (via WASM). Now:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Filters and tab state survive reloads&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The data layer is queryable&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The React tree got simpler&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tradeoff is bundle size and a slightly unusual mental model. But for a tool that people use all day, persistence is worth it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm reading about SQLite internals now because I want to understand what I'm actually shipping. The documentation is surprisingly readable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We had a settings page where props were threaded through four levels of components. Every new field touched five files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix wasn't a state library. It was composition:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SettingsSection&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Notifications"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NotificationFields&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SettingsSection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Instead of passing &lt;code&gt;value&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;onChange&lt;/code&gt; down, each section owns its slice of state, and the page composes sections. The props stayed where they were used.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We deleted about 200 lines of plumbing. The page is easier to read and the components are testable in isolation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm learning Kotlin Multiplatform next — mostly curiosity about sharing UI logic without sharing UI. If you've tried it, I'd love to compare notes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you import a &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; function into a protected React component and pass it a typed TypeScript object, it looks and feels like a private, internal function call.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It isn't. Every exported &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; function in your codebase is compiled into a &lt;strong&gt;publicly reachable HTTP &lt;code&gt;POST&lt;/code&gt; endpoint&lt;/strong&gt;. Anyone with your action's identifier can invoke it directly from &lt;code&gt;curl&lt;/code&gt;, Postman, or an automated script—with any JSON payload they want, completely bypassing your UI and often bypassing the authentication checks you thought were protecting it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your team treats Server Actions like regular TypeScript functions instead of public REST endpoints, your production app likely has an unauthenticated mutation or an authorization bug waiting to be discovered.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Illusion: Why Developers Write Vulnerable Actions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Look at this common pattern in a modern Next.js App Router codebase:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/dashboard/billing/page.tsx (Protected by Layout)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;requireAdminSession&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/lib/auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;issueRefund&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/app/actions/billing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;BillingPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ✅ The developer checks authentication in the Page or Layout&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requireAdminSession&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;form&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;use server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;issueRefund&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;invoiceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;inv_9921&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;amountCents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;submit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Issue&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;$50&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Refund&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;&amp;gt;
&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;And here is the action file it imports:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/actions/billing.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;use server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/lib/db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RefundInput&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;invoiceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;amountCents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ❌ CRITICAL VULNERABILITY: Publicly callable POST endpoint!&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;issueRefund&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RefundInput&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;invoices&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;invoiceId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;REFUNDED&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;refundedAmount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;amountCents&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;success&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;During code review, almost everyone approves this. The reasoning seems obvious:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;BillingPage&lt;/code&gt; calls &lt;code&gt;requireAdminSession()&lt;/code&gt; before rendering the button.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;issueRefund&lt;/code&gt; requires a strongly typed &lt;code&gt;RefundInput&lt;/code&gt; object.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No API route (&lt;code&gt;app/api/.../route.ts&lt;/code&gt;) was ever created.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every single one of those assumptions fails at runtime.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="crayons-card c-embed"&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The Golden Rule of Server Functions:&lt;/strong&gt; Adding &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; does not create a private server helper. It instructs the bundler to register an HTTP &lt;code&gt;POST&lt;/code&gt; handler backed by the React Flight protocol and assign it a public &lt;code&gt;Next-Action&lt;/code&gt; hash.&lt;br&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h2&gt;


Mistake #1: Assuming Layouts or Parent Pages Protect Server Actions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a user navigates to &lt;code&gt;/dashboard/billing&lt;/code&gt;, Next.js renders &lt;code&gt;layout.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;page.tsx&lt;/code&gt;. If you placed your &lt;code&gt;requireAdminSession()&lt;/code&gt; check inside &lt;code&gt;layout.tsx&lt;/code&gt;, unauthenticated visitors get redirected before seeing the UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, &lt;strong&gt;when a client invokes a Server Action, Next.js does not execute the parent &lt;code&gt;layout.tsx&lt;/code&gt; rendering logic as an authorization gate before running the action.&lt;/strong&gt; A Server Action is an independent entry point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Furthermore, relying solely on edge middleware (&lt;code&gt;middleware.ts&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;proxy.ts&lt;/code&gt; in Next.js 16) is an anti-pattern. Edge routing layers are designed for optimistic redirects and request shaping—not as your sole data-access security boundary. Even if your middleware blocks &lt;code&gt;/dashboard/*&lt;/code&gt;, a Server Action can be POSTed to &lt;strong&gt;any route in your application&lt;/strong&gt; that accepts POST requests if the attacker sends the &lt;code&gt;Next-Action&lt;/code&gt; header, or if matcher rules miss an unprotected path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
How an attacker triggers a "use server" function with curl
&lt;br&gt;
When your client bundle loads, the hashed action IDs are embedded right inside the JavaScript chunks served to the browser. Anyone inspecting the network tab or static JS files can grab the 40-character &lt;code&gt;Next-Action&lt;/code&gt; hash and send a raw HTTP request:&lt;br&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-X&lt;/span&gt; POST https://your-app.com/login &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Accept: text/x-component"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type: text/plain;charset=UTF-8"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Next-Action: 409a8b7c6d5e4f3a2b1c0d9e8f7a6b5c4d3e2f1a"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'[{"invoiceId":"inv_9921","amountCents":9999999}]'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;Notice that the request targets &lt;code&gt;/login&lt;/code&gt; (a public route) while passing the &lt;code&gt;Next-Action&lt;/code&gt; header for &lt;code&gt;issueRefund&lt;/code&gt;. Without an explicit session check &lt;strong&gt;inside&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;issueRefund&lt;/code&gt; itself, the database mutation executes immediately.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Mistake #2: Trusting TypeScript Types at Runtime
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In our vulnerable &lt;code&gt;issueRefund(input: RefundInput)&lt;/code&gt; example, the developer typed &lt;code&gt;amountCents: number&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TypeScript types are completely erased during compilation. When an HTTP request hits a Server Action endpoint, the React Server Components (RSC) Flight deserializer parses the raw request body and passes whatever the caller sent directly into your function's arguments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An attacker can send:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Negative numbers (&lt;code&gt;{"amountCents": -500000}&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Unexpected types (&lt;code&gt;{"invoiceId": {"$ne": null}, "amountCents": "NaN"}&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Massive multi-megabyte strings designed to block your event loop or bloat database columns&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even worse, framework-level deserialization bugs—such as the critical &lt;strong&gt;CVE-2025-55182&lt;/strong&gt; (CVSS 10.0) in React Server Components and &lt;strong&gt;CVE-2025-66478&lt;/strong&gt; in Next.js, followed by the May 2026 Flight parser DoS advisories—proved that Server Function endpoints sit directly on the untrusted network perimeter.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Mistake #3: Accidental Export Exposure in &lt;code&gt;"use server"&lt;/code&gt; Files
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you put &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; at the top of a file (&lt;code&gt;app/actions/users.ts&lt;/code&gt;), &lt;strong&gt;every single &lt;code&gt;export&lt;/code&gt; in that file becomes a public HTTP endpoint&lt;/strong&gt;—even internal helper functions you only exported so you could unit-test them or call them from another server file!&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/actions/users.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;use server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ❌ You exported this only for a unit test or another server module,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// but because the file has 'use server' at the top, this is now a public endpoint!&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;dangerouslyResetUserQuota&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;quotas&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;used&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;If a function does not need to be invoked directly from a Client Component or HTML &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;form&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;never export it from a &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; file&lt;/strong&gt;. Move shared server logic into a plain &lt;code&gt;.ts&lt;/code&gt; Data Access Layer (DAL) marked with &lt;code&gt;import 'server-only'&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


The Production Fix: Harden Every Action Boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To make Server Actions safe in production, treat every &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; function exactly like a public &lt;code&gt;POST /api/*&lt;/code&gt; webhook. Every action must enforce three rules inside its own body:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Authenticate inside the action&lt;/strong&gt; (never trust layouts or middleware).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Validate the runtime payload with a schema&lt;/strong&gt; (such as Zod or Valibot) using &lt;code&gt;unknown&lt;/code&gt; as the TypeScript parameter type.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Verify resource ownership (Authorization / IDOR check)&lt;/strong&gt; before mutating data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the hardened, production-ready pattern:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/actions/billing.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;use server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;zod&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/lib/db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;getVerifiedSession&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@/lib/dal/auth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 1. Define a strict runtime validation schema&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RefundSchema&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;invoiceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;uuid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;amountCents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;positive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000_00&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 2. Type the input as `unknown` so TypeScript forces you to parse it first&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;issueRefund&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rawInput&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Step A: Authenticate inside the action itself&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getVerifiedSession&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;BILLING_ADMIN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Unauthorized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Step B: Validate runtime types and constraints&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RefundSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;safeParse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rawInput&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;success&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Invalid refund payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;invoiceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;amountCents&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Step C: Enforce tenant/object ownership (Prevent IDOR)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;invoices&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;updateMany&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;invoiceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;organizationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;organizationId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Scope to caller's org!&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;PAID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;REFUNDED&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;refundedAmount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;amountCents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Invoice not found or not eligible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/data-security" class="crayons-btn crayons-btn--primary" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Read the Official Next.js Data Security and Server Actions Architecture Guide&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


Quick Production Audit Checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run through this checklist on your repository today:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Search your codebase for &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; and inspect every &lt;code&gt;export async function&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Remove &lt;code&gt;export&lt;/code&gt; from any helper function inside a &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; file that shouldn't be callable from the browser, or move it to a &lt;code&gt;server-only&lt;/code&gt; Data Access Layer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Ensure every exported Server Action calls your session/authentication verification inside its own function body.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Replace trusted TypeScript parameter types with &lt;code&gt;rawInput: unknown&lt;/code&gt; + runtime schema parsing (&lt;code&gt;zod&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;valibot&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Scope every database &lt;code&gt;UPDATE&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;DELETE&lt;/code&gt; query by the authenticated user's &lt;code&gt;userId&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;organizationId&lt;/code&gt; to prevent IDOR.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Keep &lt;code&gt;react&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;react-dom&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;next&lt;/code&gt; patched against React Flight protocol advisories (such as CVE-2025-55182 and CVE-2026-23870) and enforce request body size limits at your reverse proxy or WAF.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;


The Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ergonomics of React Server Actions are their greatest strength and their biggest security trap. By erasing the visual boundary between client code and server execution, &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; makes network boundaries invisible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never assume a layout, middleware redirect, or TypeScript interface protects a Server Action. If a file says &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt;, every exported function is a public HTTP endpoint—authenticate it, validate its payload at runtime, and authorize every database query inside the action itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Have you audited the &lt;code&gt;'use server'&lt;/code&gt; files in your production codebase recently—and did you find any exported functions missing an internal auth or schema check?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;


